# Copy to .env and fill in values for your project.
# Add variables here as the project grows so the template stays self-documenting.

# App URL (for production deployments)
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# Supabase Postgres — pooled connection (port 6543, pgbouncer=true), used by the app at runtime
DATABASE_URL=""

# Supabase Postgres — direct connection (port 5432), used only for `prisma db push`/migrations
DIRECT_URL=""

# Better Auth secret (generate with: openssl rand -base64 32)
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=""

# Better Auth base URL
BETTER_AUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# DEVELOPMENT ONLY — fixed test code for the driver app's phone sign-in.
# When this is a 6-digit value AND the server runs with NODE_ENV=development
# (`pnpm dev`), no SMS is sent and this code verifies ANY phone number.
# WARNING: anyone who can reach such a server can sign in as, or create, any
# driver. Never run it against the production database, and never expose it to
# the public internet. It is ignored under `next build`/`next start` (every
# Vercel deployment, previews included), where phone sign-in answers
# 503 SMS_NOT_CONFIGURED until a real SMS provider is wired into
# src/lib/sms/sender.ts. Leave it unset everywhere except a local dev backend.
# DEV_PHONE_OTP_CODE=""

# LocationIQ API key (used to look up addresses -> coordinates for pricing) — get one free at https://locationiq.com
LOCATIONIQ_API_KEY=""

# Google Cloud API key with the Maps JavaScript API enabled and billing active (renders the live order-tracking map; exposed to the browser, so restrict it by HTTP referrer)
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY=""

# Google Cloud API key with the Places API (New) enabled and billing active (powers the as-you-type address suggestions in the booking form).
# Server-only: it is called server-to-server and must never reach the browser — hence no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
# Keep it a separate credential from NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY above: that key is browser-exposed and HTTP-referrer-restricted, and referrer restrictions do nothing for server-side calls — restrict this one by IP instead.
GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY=""

# Supabase project URL, e.g. https://<project-ref>.supabase.co (Storage/REST base — distinct from the Postgres DATABASE_URL above)
# MANUAL SETUP: before vehicle photo uploads will work, create a **public** bucket named `vehicle-photos` in the Supabase dashboard under Storage → New bucket.
SUPABASE_URL=""

# Supabase service role key (Project Settings → API). Server-only: it bypasses row-level security, so it must never be exposed to the browser — hence no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=""

# Supabase project URL and anon (public) key (Project Settings → API), for the
# browser-side Supabase client. Both are safe to expose to the browser and are
# used only to perform a direct-to-storage upload of a driver onboarding
# document once a route handler has issued a signed upload URL (the file is too
# large to pass through a Next.js route handler body). Never put the service
# role key here.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL has the same value as SUPABASE_URL above; it needs
# to be a separate variable because Next.js only inlines the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix
# into the browser bundle. It is inlined at build time, so changing it in the
# Vercel dashboard requires a redeploy to take effect.
# MANUAL SETUP: before onboarding document uploads will work, create a
# **private** bucket named `driver-documents` in the Supabase dashboard under
# Storage → New bucket — leave "Public bucket" unchecked, unlike vehicle-photos.
# MANUAL SETUP: before proof-of-delivery uploads from the driver app will work
# (delivery photos and the recipient's signature, required to complete a job
# from the app), create a second **private** bucket named `delivery-proofs` the
# same way. It uses SUPABASE_URL and SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY above; no extra
# variable.
# MANUAL SETUP — BUCKET LIMITS (required, not optional): on BOTH private
# buckets, `driver-documents` and `delivery-proofs`, set under Storage → the
# bucket → Edit bucket:
#   - "Restrict file upload size": 10 MB
#   - "Allowed MIME types": image/jpeg, image/png
# Uploads go from the app straight to Storage on a signed URL, so the server
# never sees the bytes; it checks type and size only when an upload is
# *registered*. An upload that is made and never registered is checked by the
# bucket alone — without these limits it can be any type and up to the
# project's global maximum. The server separately caps how many such uploads
# can be outstanding per order / per vehicle and deletes them when their URL
# expires. The values are `REQUIRED_BUCKET_LIMITS` in src/lib/uploads/rules.ts.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=""

# Driver support phone line, shown in the driver app as its "Call driver
# support" button (served to signed-in drivers by GET /api/dashboard/hub/me as
# `supportPhone`). Optional and absent by default: leave it unset and the app
# hides the call button — there is no fallback number. Written exactly as it
# should be dialled, e.g. an E.164 number. Server-only and read at request
# time, so changing it needs no rebuild.
# DRIVER_SUPPORT_PHONE=""

# Push notifications to the driver app, sent through Expo's push service
# (https://docs.expo.dev/push-notifications/sending-notifications/). Used for
# pushed load offers and route alerts: a notification carrying only ids and
# generic wording, never an address, name or amount. Optional and OFF by default: with
# EXPO_PUSH_ENABLED unset (or anything but "true") nothing is ever sent and the
# app finds offers by polling GET /api/dashboard/hub/offers/current instead.
# Server-only and read at request time, so changing these needs no rebuild.
# EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN is needed only if "Enhanced Security for Push
# Notifications" is switched on for the Expo project (EAS dashboard → project →
# Credentials); it is sent as a Bearer token. Sending itself needs no key — the
# app's Expo push credentials (APNs key, FCM) are configured in EAS, not here.
# EXPO_PUSH_API_URL points sending at a stand-in for local testing and is
# IGNORED when NODE_ENV=production; leave it unset everywhere else.
# EXPO_PUSH_ENABLED="true"
# EXPO_ACCESS_TOKEN=""
# EXPO_PUSH_API_URL=""

# Merchant/client host split — set to the exact hostname (including port for
# local dev) that should serve DRIVER/COMPANY sign-in, sign-up, and the ops
# dashboard, fully separated from the CLIENT-facing main domain. Tri-state:
# leave unset/blank to disable the split entirely (every role shares one
# host, exactly as this template behaves without this var). Local dev value:
# "merchant.localhost:3000" — works with no /etc/hosts edit in Chrome, Edge,
# and Firefox (they resolve *.localhost to loopback natively); Safari on
# macOS does not do this reliably before macOS Tahoe, see README for a
# workaround. Production value: the real merchant subdomain, e.g.
# "merchant.example.com" — set this in Vercel's Production environment only;
# leave Preview and Development unset, since preview deployment URLs have no
# stable subdomain structure to split against.
# NOTE: this value is inlined into the client bundle at build time — changing
# it in the Vercel dashboard requires a redeploy to take effect.
NEXT_PUBLIC_MERCHANT_HOST=""

# Admin back-office host — set to the exact hostname (including port for local
# dev) that should serve the internal `/admin` back office, fully separated
# from both the client and merchant hosts. Tri-state and completely
# independent of NEXT_PUBLIC_MERCHANT_HOST above: leave unset/blank to disable
# the admin host split, in which case `/admin` stays reachable path-based on
# the main host (gated by the ADMIN role guard either way). Local dev value:
# "admin.localhost:3000" — works with no /etc/hosts edit in Chrome, Edge, and
# Firefox (they resolve *.localhost to loopback natively); Safari on macOS does
# not do this reliably before macOS Tahoe, see README for a workaround.
# Production value: the real admin subdomain, e.g. "admin.example.com" — set
# this in Vercel's Production environment only; leave Preview and Development
# unset, since preview deployment URLs have no stable subdomain structure to
# split against. Must not equal the client host (derived from BETTER_AUTH_URL)
# or NEXT_PUBLIC_MERCHANT_HOST — the split disables itself with an error log if
# it does.
# NOTE: this value is inlined into the client bundle at build time — changing
# it in the Vercel dashboard requires a redeploy to take effect.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ADMIN_HOST=""
